* Horn-loaded loudspeaker, large signal: the nonlinear suspension and motor distort a 30 Hz tone.
*
* The system of speaker_ac.net (64-section horn), driven by 20 V at 30 Hz, below the horn's
* 40 Hz cutoff where the cone moves most. Two copies:
*   X1  nonlinear: the suspension stiffens (K doubles at 6 mm) and Bl falls (halves at 10 mm);
*   X2  linear: the same driver with xk and xbl at 1 km.
* Checks, in docs/examples/multiphysics/speaker_plots.py: the harmonic content of the mouth
* pressure (odd harmonics only, the nonlinearities being symmetric), and an energy balance over
* the last ten cycles: electrical power in = Joule heat + mechanical loss + radiated power.
* What to look for (measured at 2.16.2+3), over the last ten cycles:
*   X1: peak excursion 6.47 mm, its 3rd harmonic 2.3 %. But the mouth pressure's 3rd harmonic
*       (7.39 Pa) nearly equals its fundamental (7.86 Pa): the horn radiates 90 Hz far better
*       than 30 Hz, below its cutoff. And the coil takes 55 x the linear driver's Joule heat
*       (2.10 J against 0.038 J): Bl falls with excursion, and the back-EMF that held the
*       current down at the system's 30 Hz resonance goes with it.
*   X2: 6.66 mm, the small-signal value exactly; no harmonics.
*   Energy balance, in = Joule + mechanical + radiated: residual -0.002 % (X1), +0.001 % (X2).
* Plot: python docs/examples/multiphysics/speaker_plots.py target/release/va-cli
* Run: va-cli sim circuits/multiphysics/speaker_large.net --model models/multiphysics/speaker --tran --report m1,m2,X1.x,X2.x
V1 p1 gnd SIN(0 20 30)
V2 p2 gnd SIN(0 20 30)
VA amb gnd DC 293.15
X1 p1 gnd t1 th1 driver
XC1 t1 chamber
XH1 t1 m1 horn
XM1 m1 mouth
XT1 th1 amb vc_thermal
X2 p2 gnd t2 th2 driver xk=1e3 xbl=1e3
XC2 t2 chamber
XH2 t2 m2 horn
XM2 m2 mouth
XT2 th2 amb vc_thermal
.tran 50u 0.6
.end
